      "effect": "advances",
      "direction": null,
      "quote": "A landlord may raise rent by any amount",
      "mechanism": "Expands the control property owners have over their rental units by removing state-imposed limits on pricing.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "concern_id": "C17",
      "effect": "costs",
      "direction": null,
      "quote": "The limit on residential rent increases is repealed.",
      "mechanism": "Eliminates the legal restriction that shielded renters from annual rent increases exceeding 5 percent.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    }
  ],
  "analysis": [
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Current law limits residential rent increases to no more than 5 percent within a 12-month period for units older than 15 years. This measure removes that cap entirely, requiring only a 60-day written notice for rent increases of any size.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "Landlords and property owners benefit from the ability to increase rents without legal maximums. Renters bear the cost through potentially higher housing expenses and decreased financial predictability.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Landlords are likely to raise rents more aggressively during periods of high demand or inflation to match market rates. Some renters facing steep increases may be forced to relocate, increasing housing turnover. The removal of price controls may also incentivize developers to build new rental properties or landlords to invest in building maintenance.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The measure addresses the limitation landlords face in adjusting rent prices to cover inflation, rising maintenance costs, or market conditions. By removing this limit, it also removes a mechanism designed to protect tenants from unequal bargaining power and sudden spikes in housing costs.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "answer": "Landlords and property management companies gain total discretion over residential rent pricing.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q7",
      "answer": "The economic effects of removing rent control are heavily debated. Researchers generally agree that removing caps leads to higher rental costs and increased displacement for existing tenants in the short term. However, many economists also find that removing rent control increases the overall housing supply and encourages property maintenance over the long term, whereas strict rent caps can lead to housing shortages and deterioration.",
      "disputed": true
    }
  ],
